Make the branch gate provably fail, and measure init's signing path (#72, #134) (#157)

* fix(ci): add red-then-green self-test for the branch coverage gate and record ADR-0043 (#72)

RM-064's acceptance criteria required the branch floor's self-test to
demonstrate red-then-green, not only the statement floor's, and required an
ADR recording the gobco choice. scripts/branch-coverage.sh,
COVERAGE_REQUIRE_PACKAGES=1 and the removal of the NOT ENFORCED block were
already done and are verified working, not touched here.

Adds scripts/branch-coverage-selftest.sh, shaped like
coverage-floors-selftest.sh: builds a throwaway module with an uncovered
three-condition branch plus a fully-covered control surface, asserts
branch-coverage.sh reports the ledger surface FAILed and the control surface
ok (exit 1), covers the branch, and asserts both surfaces ok (exit 0). Wires
it into .github/workflows/ci.yml as its own job, branch-coverage-gate-selftest.

ADR-0043 records why gobco's condition coverage cannot be replaced by
statement coverage, why it floors five files across the four IP §2 surfaces,
and why it runs as a separate CI job: gobco reruns the tests rather than
reading an existing profile, so folding it into the statement-coverage job
would rerun internal/verify's and internal/mcp's container-backed suites a
second time for no benefit.

* fix(ci): stop branch-coverage comments claiming verify/mcp do not exist (#72)

internal/verify (RM-037) and internal/mcp (RM-022..025) have landed and both
report ok at 100% branch coverage, not PENDING — the header comments in
branch-coverage.sh and coverage-floors.sh still said otherwise. Points both
at ADR-0043 for the mechanism rationale instead.

* docs(adr): index ADR-0042, which was added to the notes and not the table

* test(cli): add a real-stack integration test for init's signing path (#134)

spireSignVerifier.Run and createVerificationCommit in cmd/innsegl/initsign.go
sat at 0% coverage: every other layer of `innsegl init` is tested against
real temporary git repositories and a real HTTP checksum fetch, but the one
step #117 exists to guarantee - "setup is finished because the chain held" -
had no measurement behind it.

Adds INIT-008 (happy path) and INIT-009 (a real misconfiguration is refused)
against a real containerised SPIRE, Fulcio and Rekor, combining
internal/signing's harness shape with internal/mcp's admin-proxy-over-socat
and test/smoke's PEM-file admin credential so spireSignVerifier.Run can
spire.Dial the admin API from the test process itself.

The stack costs ~8 Docker networks per run (#100's address-pool ceiling), so
it is gated behind INNSEGL_RUN_INIT_E2E and does not run inside the default
`go test ./...` or CI matrix - "make the test schedulable" rather than
"reuse an existing stack", since cmd/innsegl has no existing SPIRE/Sigstore
stack and ADR-0022 already rejected sharing one across processes.

Driven with TDD: run first against a deliberately wrong OIDC issuer,
observed a real Fulcio-side certificate rejection, then reverted and
observed a real pass. Coverage of the two target functions: Run 0% -> 84.0%,
createVerificationCommit 0% -> 69.0%.

Boundary: this proves everything downstream of credential acquisition -
SPIRE admin dial, JWT-SVID minting, gitsign, Fulcio issuance, the Rekor
entry, worktree isolation, trailers, and a second independent `innsegl
verify` - through the file-based bootstrap branch of
openInitCredentialSource. It does not and cannot exercise the interactive
OIDC browser login itself, which #117 says is the actual identity proof;
that remains provable only by a human running the command.

